────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ☐ multiSelect

│ 30 of the 425 asks on disk carry a multiSelect question, where the widget wants space-to-toggle then enter. How should
│ the phone drive that?

❯ 1. One tap = one toggle, then a separate done (Recommended)
     Tapping an option sends 'step to it + space' and nothing else. The next poll re-reads the pane, which paints the
     toggle state per row, so the phone renders checked options as checked from the screen rather than from local
     memory. A separate 'done' button sends enter. Costs a 4s poll per toggle.
  2. Collect locally, send one sequence
     The phone tracks which options you ticked and, on 'done', sends the whole script: step, space, step, space, enter.
     One round trip and no poll latency between taps, which matters when you are ticking three of four options. But it
     is an intermediate state that is never re-read, so one dropped keystroke silently mis-toggles everything after it.
  3. Send multiSelect to free text
     Render the options read-only and answer through the widget's free-text row in prose. No toggle arithmetic at all,
     and multiSelect stops being a distinct code path. But it converts a structured answer into text the receiving
     Claude has to re-interpret, and 30 of 425 is frequent enough that this would be a visible downgrade rather than an
     edge case.
  4. Type something.
